
A couple deliberate choices there: I **would keep the AI-assisted development section**. It matches the philosophy you've already established without making the README sound like "AI built this for me." It frames AI as part of your tooling while making ownership explicit.

I also wouldn't call this merely a "training project." Unlike `hello-keizoku` and the task tracker, this has evolved into an actual studio website that simultaneously serves as a development project. The README reflects both.

---

## `.gitignore`

For the current project, we don't need a 200-line boilerplate `.gitignore`.

```gitignore
# =========================================================
# KEIZOKU DEVOPS
# Git Ignore
# =========================================================


# ---------- Dependencies ----------

node_modules/


# ---------- Environment Files ----------

.env
.env.*
!.env.example


# ---------- Build / Distribution ----------

dist/
build/
.cache/
.parcel-cache/


# ---------- Logs ----------

*.log
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
pnpm-debug.log*


# ---------- Operating System ----------

.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
Desktop.ini


# ---------- Editor / IDE ----------

.vscode/
.idea/

*.code-workspace


# ---------- Temporary Files ----------

*.tmp
*.temp
*.swp
*.swo

*~
~$*


# ---------- Test / Coverage Output ----------

coverage/


# ---------- Local Development ----------

.local/
temp/
tmp/